# Trivy IaC suppressions.
#
# Each entry below is an explicit acceptance, not a "fix later". The
# common thread is: these checks recommend hardening that costs more
# than it saves for the threat model of this small e-commerce site
# (one product line, low single-digit orders/week, no card data on
# our servers — PayFast handles that). See docs/security.md for the
# canonical risk register.
#
# When adding a suppression: include the AVD ID, a one-line summary,
# and one or two sentences on why we accept the risk. Future audits
# can re-evaluate per-line without re-deriving the rationale.

# S3 bucket does not encrypt data with a customer-managed KMS key.
# The frontend bucket is static, public-read assets (HTML, CSS, JS,
# images of for-sale products) — there is nothing in it worth a
# $1/month CMK + per-API-call cost. SSE-S3 (AES-256 with an
# AWS-managed key) is enabled by default and adequate.
AVD-AWS-0132

# S3 bucket has logging disabled. Access logging would charge for
# log storage + processing on a static asset bucket whose access
# patterns we don't audit at this scale. CloudFront in front of it
# is the choke point; if we ever needed traffic insight we'd add
# CloudFront logging (suppressed below for the same reason).
AVD-AWS-0089

# CloudFront distribution does not utilise a WAF. AWS WAF costs $5+
# per month per Web ACL plus per-request fees — disproportionate
# for a site of this scale. docs/privacy lists DDoS protection as
# explicitly out of scope, and the privacy policy reflects that.
AVD-AWS-0011

# CloudFront distribution does not have logging enabled. Same
# rationale as the S3 bucket logging suppression — log volume on
# a site of this scale is not worth the cost of storage +
# processing without a concrete need to inspect access patterns.
AVD-AWS-0010

# Lambda function does not have tracing enabled. AWS X-Ray costs
# per request invocation. The Lambda's HTTP handlers complete in
# <1s and have synchronous logs to CloudWatch; we don't need
# distributed tracing for a single-Lambda backend.
AVD-AWS-0066

# CloudWatch log group is not encrypted with a customer-managed
# key. Logs contain orderRefs + status changes — no PII (the admin
# routes are regression-guarded to log only orderRef + action +
# result). AWS-managed encryption at rest is the default; CMK
# would add cost without protecting anything customer-identifying.
AVD-AWS-0017

# DynamoDB table encryption uses the default AWS-managed
# `aws/dynamodb` KMS key rather than a customer-managed key.
# Documented as a deliberate choice in infra/README.md — same
# blast-radius rationale: PII rotation lives in the application
# layer (365-day TTL), CMK would add cost without changing the
# trust boundary.
AVD-AWS-0025

# API Gateway stage does not have access logging configured.
# Same rationale as CloudFront access logging — the backend is
# logged via CloudWatch Logs from the Lambda itself. API Gateway
# access logs would be redundant for traffic insight and would
# carry the customer email URL param for retry calls (a privacy
# downgrade we explicitly avoid — see docs/security.md § Risk 3).
AVD-AWS-0001
